{"name":"White Scar","hex":"#8c9fa1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"scar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A permanent mark on the skin, sometimes caused by the healing of a wound.","A permanent negative effect on someone's mind, caused by a traumatic experience."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark the skin permanently.","To form a scar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scar","blocks":[{"text":"A scar is an area of fibrous tissue that replaces normal skin following an injury. Scars result from the biological process of wound repair in the skin, as well as in other organs and tissues of the body. Thus, scarring is a natural part of the healing process. With the exception of very minor lesions, every wound results in some degree of scarring. An exception is animals with complete regeneration, which regrow tissue without scar formation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Scar_%28xndr%29.jpg/330px-Scar_%28xndr%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scar"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg/500px-Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg/1280px-Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg/500px-Novak_7_%285451438566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Man with visible facial scars","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANovak_7_(5451438566).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Atrophic_scar_of_Ehlers_Danlos_syndrome.jpg/500px-Atrophic_scar_of_Ehlers_Danlos_syndrome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Atrophic_scar_of_Ehlers_Danlos_syndrome.jpg/500px-Atrophic_scar_of_Ehlers_Danlos_syndrome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Atrophic \"cigarette paper\" scar in Ehlers–Danlos patient","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtrophic_scar_of_Ehlers_Danlos_syndrome.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Key_processes_contributing_to_the_quasi-neoplastic_expression_of_keloid_pathobiology.jpg/500px-Key_processes_contributing_to_the_quasi-neoplastic_expression_of_keloid_pathobiology.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm